Z||000000||Kathrin||05-11-2001||02:53 PM||||::Frowns::  I have no idea where to start looking for the problem in my car.  Someone tells me the alternator, electric problems, fuel pump, and im completely lost!  She died on me one day, and i had her jumped.  She ran for a few minutes, then died again. I went an got a new battery, and she started up just fine after that.  I made the 15 mile drive home just fine, then another drive the next day.  That night tho, i started her up, and drove about 5 miles, when all the lights in the car went out, console, headlights, radio, and whatnot.  Less then a minute later, she died.  3 days later, i went back to get her, and she started fine, and made the drive home.  Its been 3 weeks now, and she wont start.  Last time she did this, i put her in the shop, and they charged me almost 300$ to replace a burnt wire, only to have the same thing happen a month later. If anyone has any clue one what could be the problem, without having to take her in, that would be great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks ::smiles::||||reg||9
Z||000001||Haspers8||05-11-2001||03:04 PM||||  Alternator maybe???? Sounds like it may not be charging......but I am sure the Fiero gods will know.....<P>Welcome to the board.  Much fun here.<P>Later <BR>Steve LT1 IMSA||||reg||6
Z||000002||RacinRob||05-11-2001||03:19 PM||||Clean the battery cables? I had a similar problem when I took mine out of storage, all it was, was rusty connections.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||05-11-2001||03:22 PM||||Sounds like an alternator problem to me. You should take it and get it checked. I have an O'Reillys near me and they check stuff like that for free.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||05-11-2001||03:38 PM||||definately sounds like your altinator is not charging the battery for some reason. when a battery goes dead like that while driving it might come back up after sitting for a while enough to start it again.<P>  but then again it could just be a bad connection to the battery or the fuse box, which seems a little more likely, its hard to say. <P>  Is the car dead now? can you get your hands on a voltmeter and check the voltage on the battery.<P>  If the battery is at 12V (DC) or more, and the car wont start, and the lights dont go on, then the connections between the battery and the rest of the car are bad.<P>  If the battery is low (less than 12 volts) and if you can recharge the battery (either with a recharger or have someone jump it for 10 minutes with their engine running to charge your battery back up) and then your car starts up okay, but after a while the battery voltage drops again and the car quits, then the altinator is not charging the battery. either a bad altinator, or a bad wire connection between the altinator and the battery.<P>In fact after you start your car the battery voltage should go up to 13.8 to 15 Volts if the altinator is charging the battery. If it stays at 12V or less its not being charged by the altinator.<P>  If the battery stays at 12V or more all the time, and the car just quits, then you have a loose connection somewhere in the wiring of the car.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mark||05-11-2001||05:09 PM||||You said "I made the 15 mile drive home just fine, then another drive the next day. That night tho, i started her up, and drove about 5 miles, when all the lights in the car went out, console, headlights, radio, and whatnot".  I would say you have an intermittent electrical connection someplace.  These can be the worst to track down.  I would suggest you get a voltmeter and carry it with you.  If the car is dead right now, so much the better since you should be able to find the problem.  <P>Pay particular attention to the battery cables, fusable links, and ground straps (they do loosen up!)||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||05-11-2001||05:43 PM||||Which wire was burnt?  You may have a dead short somewhere and the mechanic fixed the symptom but not the root cause.  Another thing to check is the power distribution block in the eng bay on the passenger side.  Make sure all of those connections are clean.  As a matter of fact, take it apart clean the connections and put new dielectric grease in it.  Another possibility is the fusible link that is in the same area.  That wire is actually fuse material and can deteriorate inside the wire casing cause opens.<P>GL<P>Edited to say Welcome to PFF.  It is a great community here and should be able to get lots of help.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||1
